editorialMd:
Build the result list

This graph has two-way links between points. A group, also called a connected component, contains all points that can reach one another. Start at point 0, then check the other points in number order. Whenever a point has not been visited, explore its whole group depth first: follow a path as far as you can before going back. Record how many points are in that group. The result list follows the order in which you first find the groups. A graph with no points gives an empty result list.

Return the answer

Count the even numbers in the result list. An even number divides by 2 with no remainder. Count 0 and negative even numbers too. Return the count. An empty list gives 0.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(V^2); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the input length; V is the number of graph points. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function islandLedgersEvenKeyCensus(links, k) {
  const a=links; let out=[];
  const n=a.length,seen=Array(n).fill(false);for(let start=0;start<n;start++){if(seen[start])continue;const stack=[start];seen[start]=true;let count=0;while(stack.length){const u=stack.pop();count++;for(let v=0;v<n;v++)if(a[u][v]&&!seen[v]){seen[v]=true;stack.push(v);}}out.push(count);}
  return out.filter(x=>x%2===0).length;
}


